 # Protocol Reverse Engineering
 
 Comprehensive techniques for capturing, analyzing, and documenting network protocols for security research, interoperability, and debugging.
 
+## Scope And Authorization
+
+Read this before running anything in this skill.
+
+- **Passive analysis is the default.** Reading a capture file the user gave you, or dissecting their own app's traffic, needs no extra step.
+- **Capture only traffic you are allowed to see:** the user's own devices, apps, and lab networks, or systems they say they are authorized to test. Never capture on a shared or public network, or traffic belonging to other people.
+- **Active testing needs explicit confirmation every time.** Before transparent interception, `--ssl-insecure`, fuzzing, or replaying or sending packets (`send()`, Scapy, boofuzz), name the exact target host and port and ask the user to confirm they own or are authorized to test it. Never point these at production or third-party services unless the user says they are in scope.
+- **Credentials and secrets:** don't extract, store, or reuse credentials, session tokens, or TLS keys belonging to anyone but the user. Keep captures and key logs in the project's `tmp/` folder, never commit them, and delete them when the work is done.
+
